  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why are glaciers called water towers?",
      "a": "Glaciers store water as ice in the cold season and release it as meltwater in the warm, dry season, like a tank on a tower. This keeps rivers flowing when there is little rain.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a glacier?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Who depends on glacier meltwater?",
      "a": "Farmers, cities and hydropower stations downstream. Rivers that start in the Himalaya and nearby ranges supply well over a billion people, and in the Andes cities like La Paz and Lima use glacier water too.",
      "asks": [
        "Which rivers start in the Himalayas?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are glaciers shrinking?",
      "a": "In a warmer climate, more ice melts each summer than new snow adds each winter. Most glaciers around the world are shrinking because of climate change.",
      "asks": [
        "Is climate change melting glaciers?",
        "What happens when glaciers melt?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is peak water?",
      "a": "As a glacier shrinks, its meltwater first increases, reaches a peak, and then falls because there is less ice left to melt. Rivers fed by small glaciers may already be past their peak.",
      "asks": [
        "Will rivers run dry when glaciers melt?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is an ice stupa?",
      "a": "An ice stupa is an artificial glacier made in Ladakh, India. In winter, water is sprayed into freezing air and builds a tall cone of ice, which melts slowly in spring to water crops.",
      "asks": [
        "How do people make artificial glaciers?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Rivers that start in the Hindu Kush Himalaya supply water to well over a billion people.",
    "The Himalaya and nearby ranges hold the largest store of ice outside the polar regions, often called the Third Pole.",
    "Ice stupas, artificial glaciers for farm water, were developed in Ladakh, India, in the 2010s."
  ],
